{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-110368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "21d9fc729b9b789500ef95adb1d3b43ee54db9739545e640fdd43b4b94f44262"} {"question" "q-110368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{21}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110368" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{21}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>0.5,Y>0.5}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/7e81ce16404bc6c5c826f79a88519bd6216156f5bb0f8af1eae41f19ca495de5.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "21d9fc729b9b789500ef95adb1d3b43ee54db9739545e640fdd43b4b94f44262"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{16}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110370" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cxy+\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{cx}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}+\\frac{c}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "21d9fc729b9b789500ef95adb1d3b43ee54db9739545e640fdd43b4b94f44262"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x<2y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "21d9fc729b9b789500ef95adb1d3b43ee54db9739545e640fdd43b4b94f44262"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-110977" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-111162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-111162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},$ and the event $A={X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/assets/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7feeeb"} {"question" "q-111162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "21d9fc729b9b789500ef95adb1d3b43ee54db9739545e640fdd43b4b94f44262"} {"question" "q-111162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069972/state.json" "file_sha256" "d053cb8b68b73a2d443a2ecf1efb3d14142ce56ccd07f9beb53f466e7f7f